  "guidebook": {
    "intro": "Family in East Africa is wide: cousins are brothers and sisters, any older woman can be mama, and a grandmother’s word carries weight.",
    "phrases": [
      { "target": "Huyu ni mama yangu.", "base": "This is my mother." },
      { "target": "Huyu ni nani?", "base": "Who is this?" },
      { "target": "Una watoto?", "base": "Do you have children?" },
      { "target": "Nina watoto watatu.", "base": "I have three children." },
      { "target": "Sina dada.", "base": "I don’t have a sister." },
      { "target": "Familia yangu ni nzuri.", "base": "My family is lovely." },
      { "target": "Bibi ni mtu mzuri.", "base": "Grandmother is a good person." }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "my-family",
        "title": "My, your, his, her",
        "body": [
          "“My” comes after the noun. For people it is usually **wangu**, **wako**, **wake**: **rafiki wangu** (my friend), **mwalimu wako** (your teacher), **mjukuu wake** (his or her grandchild).",
          "With close family words (**mama**, **baba**, **kaka**, **dada**, **bibi**, **babu**) many speakers use the **y-** forms instead: **mama yangu**, **baba yako**. You’ll hear both; this course uses **yangu** with family and **wangu** with other people."
        ],
        "table": [
          ["English", "People", "Close family"],
          ["my", "wangu", "yangu"],
          ["your", "wako", "yako"],
          ["his, her", "wake", "yake"]
        ],
        "targetColumns": [1, 2]
      },
      {
        "id": "nina-sina",
        "title": "Nina and sina",
        "body": [
          "“To have” is **-na** with a person in front: **nina** (I have), **una** (you have), **ana** (he or she has).",
          "To say you don’t have something, change the start: **sina** (I don’t have), **huna** (you don’t have), **hana** (he or she doesn’t have)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "m-wa",
        "title": "People nouns: m- and wa-",
        "body": [
          "Swahili sorts nouns into classes, each with its own prefix. Most words for people start with **m-** for one and **wa-** for more: **mtu**, **watu**; **mtoto**, **watoto**; **mwalimu**, **walimu**.",
          "Describing words and numbers copy the prefix: **mtu mzuri** (a good person), **watu wazuri** (good people), **watoto watatu** (three children)."
        ],
        "table": [
          ["One", "Many", "English"],
          ["mtu", "watu", "person, people"],
          ["mtoto", "watoto", "child, children"],
          ["mtu mzuri", "watu wazuri", "a good person, good people"]
        ]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Mama and baba for everyone",
        "body": [
          "It is polite to call an older woman **mama** and an older man **baba** or **mzee**, even a stranger. Parents are often called after their first child: **Mama Neema** is Neema’s mother."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Bibi’s proverbs",
        "body": [
          "Grandparents are famous for **methali** (proverbs). You met one already: **Haraka haraka haina baraka**, “hurry, hurry has no blessing”."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
